                    "id": "random-vectors",
                    "label": "Random Vectors & Transformations",
                    "content": "## Random Vectors, Joint Distributions & Transformations\n\n### Joint, Marginal & Conditional Distributions\n\n**Joint CDF:** \\(F(x,y) = P(X \\le x,\\, Y \\le y)\\)\n\n**Joint PDF (continuous):** \\(f(x,y) \\ge 0\\), \\(\\displaystyle\\int\\!\\int f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy = 1\\)\n\n**Marginal PDFs:**\n\\[\nf_X(x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x,y)\\,dy, \\qquad f_Y(y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x,y)\\,dx\n\\]\n\n**Conditional PDF:**\n\\[\nf(x|y) = \\frac{f(x,y)}{f_Y(y)}, \\quad f_Y(y) > 0\n\\]\n\n**Independence:** \\(X \\perp Y \\iff f(x,y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y(y)\\) for all \\(x,y\\)\n\n**Covariance & Correlation:**\n\\[\n\\text{Cov}(X,Y) = E(XY) - E(X)E(Y)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\rho = \\frac{\\text{Cov}(X,Y)}{\\sigma_X \\sigma_Y}, \\quad -1 \\le \\rho \\le 1\n\\]\nIndependence \\(\\Rightarrow\\) \\(\\rho = 0\\), but \\(\\rho = 0\\) does NOT imply independence (except for Normal).\n\n---\n\n### Functions of Random Variables\n\n**CDF method (direct):** For \\(Y = g(X)\\):\n\\[\nF_Y(y) = P(g(X) \\le y)\n\\]\nthen differentiate to get \\(f_Y\\).\n\n**Jacobian method (transformation):** If \\(Y = g(X)\\) is monotone:\n\\[\nf_Y(y) = f_X\\!\\left(g^{-1}(y)\\right) \\cdot \\left|\\frac{dx}{dy}\\right|\n\\]\n\n**Bivariate Jacobian:** For \\((U,V) = g(X,Y)\\), one-to-one:\n\\[\nf_{U,V}(u,v) = f_{X,Y}(x,y) \\cdot |J|^{-1}\n\\]\nwhere the Jacobian determinant is:\n\\[\nJ = \\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\partial x/\\partial u & \\partial x/\\partial v \\\\ \\partial y/\\partial u & \\partial y/\\partial v \\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Key Transformation Results (ISS High-Yield)\n\n| If... | Then... |\n|---|---|\n| \\(X \\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\) | \\(aX+b \\sim N(a\\mu+b,\\, a^2\\sigma^2)\\) |\n| \\(X_i \\sim N(\\mu_i,\\sigma_i^2)\\) independent | \\(\\sum a_i X_i \\sim N(\\sum a_i\\mu_i,\\, \\sum a_i^2\\sigma_i^2)\\) |\n| \\(Z \\sim N(0,1)\\) | \\(Z^2 \\sim \\chi^2(1)\\) |\n| \\(X \\sim \\chi^2(m),\\, Y \\sim \\chi^2(n)\\) indep. | \\(X+Y \\sim \\chi^2(m+n)\\) |\n| \\(X \\sim \\text{Gamma}(\\alpha,\\lambda)\\) | \\(cX \\sim \\text{Gamma}(\\alpha,\\, \\lambda/c)\\) |\n| \\(X \\sim \\text{Exp}(\\lambda)\\) | \\(X \\sim \\text{Gamma}(1,\\lambda)\\) |\n| \\(X \\sim U(0,1)\\) | \\(-\\ln X \\sim \\text{Exp}(1)\\) |\n| \\(X \\sim N(0,1),\\, V \\sim \\chi^2(n)\\) indep. | \\(X/\\sqrt{V/n} \\sim t_n\\) |\n\n---\n\n### MGF of Sum of Independent RVs\n\n\\[\nM_{X+Y}(t) = M_X(t) \\cdot M_Y(t) \\quad \\text{if } X \\perp Y\n\\]\n\nThis is the primary tool for proving reproductive properties of distributions.\n\n> **ISS tip:** The Jacobian transformation is one of the most tested topics. Always check if the transformation is one-to-one. For non-monotone \\(g\\), split the domain. The sum of independent normals/Poissons/Gammas reproductive property is almost certainly tested every year."
