            "tips": "**High-priority topics for ISS Statistical Inference section (Paper II):**\n\n- **MLE properties:** Consistency, asymptotic normality, invariance (\\(g(\\hat{\\theta})\\) is MLE of \\(g(\\theta)\\)), asymptotic efficiency — all guaranteed MCQs\n- **MLE of \\(\\sigma^2\\):** Uses divisor \\(n\\) (biased); unbiased estimator uses \\(n-1\\) — classic MCQ trap\n- **CRLB:** \\(\\text{Var}(T) \\ge 1/[nI(\\theta)]\\) for unbiased \\(T\\); does NOT apply when support depends on \\(\\theta\\) (e.g., \\(U(0,\\theta)\\))\n- **Fisher information:** \\(I(\\theta) = E[S^2] = -E[\\partial^2\\ln f/\\partial\\theta^2]\\); for Normal: \\(I(\\theta) = 1/\\sigma^2\\)\n- **Factorisation theorem:** \\(f(\\mathbf{x};\\theta) = g(T(\\mathbf{x}),\\theta)\\cdot h(\\mathbf{x})\\) — practise for all standard distributions\n- **UMVUE strategy:** Complete sufficient statistic + Rao–Blackwell + Lehmann–Scheffé\n- **Rao–Blackwell:** \\(E(U|T)\\) is at least as good as \\(U\\); reduces variance without bias\n- **NP Lemma:** MP test rejects when \\(L(\\theta_1)/L(\\theta_0) > k\\) — simple vs simple hypotheses only\n- **UMP test:** Exists for one-sided alternatives in MLR families (Karlin–Rubin); does NOT exist for two-sided alternatives in general\n- **Wilks' theorem:** \\(-2\\ln\\Lambda \\sim \\chi^2_r\\) where \\(r\\) = number of restrictions\n- **SPRT boundaries:** \\(A = \\beta/(1-\\alpha)\\), \\(B = (1-\\beta)/\\alpha\\); ASN at \\(H_0\\) and \\(H_1\\) both minimised simultaneously (Wald–Wolfowitz)\n- **OC function:** Probability of ACCEPTING \\(H_0\\); ASN = expected sample size\n- **Bayes estimators:** SEL → posterior mean; absolute loss → posterior median; 0-1 → posterior mode\n- **Bhattacharya bounds:** Order-1 = CRLB; increasing order gives tighter bounds\n- **Bootstrap:** Resamples WITH replacement; bias = \\(\\bar{\\theta}^* - \\hat{\\theta}\\)\n- **Jackknife:** Leave-one-out; bias = \\((n-1)(\\bar{\\theta}_{(.)} - \\hat{\\theta})\\) — the \\((n-1)\\) factor is tested directly\n- **Type I vs II:** Decreasing \\(\\alpha\\) without increasing \\(n\\) always increases \\(\\beta\\)\n- **Similar region:** Power function = \\(\\alpha\\) (constant) on \\(H_0\\), not just \\(\\le \\alpha\\)"
